>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 258 0 R
/E (Hemispheric Patterns: Similar age-related associations were observed across hemispheres, providing preliminary evidence of comparable patterns of age-related variation in bilateral insular thickness.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [72]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 259 0 R
/E (Sex-Based Differences: No statistically significant sex-based differences in age-adjusted insular cortical thickness were detected. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [73]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 36 0 R
/T (Limitations & Future Research)
/E (Limitations & Future Research)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [74]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 260 0 R
/E (Sample Size: The small sample \(N = 36\) limits statistical power, precision, and generalizability. Larger, more diverse samples are needed to evaluate the robustness of the observed associations and better characterize individual variability.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [75]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (Developmental Trajectories: The cross-sectional design captures age-related differences at one time point rather than within-person change. Longitudinal studies could clarify how insular cortical thickness changes throughout childhood and adolescence.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [76]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 262 0 R
/E (Future Directions: Future research could examine potential hemispheric and sex-related differences in larger samples and integrate cortical thickness measures with diffusion MRI and tractography to investigate relationships between insular anatomy and structural connectivity.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [77]
>>
endobj
